      "text": "The formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is given by:\n\\[\na_n = a_1 + (n-1)d\n\\]\nwhere $a_n$ is the $n$th term, $a_1$ is the first term, and $d$ is the common difference.\n\nGiven that the fifth term ($a_5$) is 11 and the common difference ($d$) is 1, we can set up the equation for the fifth term:\n\\[\na_5 = a_1 + (5-1)d\n\\]\n\\[\n11 = a_1 + 4 \\cdot 1\n\\]\n\\[\n11 = a_1 + 4\n\\]\nSolving for $a_1$:\n\\[\na_1 = 11 - 4\n\\]\n\\[\na_1 = 7\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find the product of the first two terms of the sequence. The first term is $a_1$ and the second term is $a_2$. Using the formula for the $n$th term again, we find $a_2$:\n\\[\na_2 = a_1 + (2-1)d\n\\]\n\\[\na_2 = 7 + 1 \\cdot 1\n\\]\n\\[\na_2 = 7 + 1\n\\]\n\\[\na_2 = 8\n\\]\n\nThe product of the first two terms is:\n\\[\na_1 \\cdot a_2 = 7 \\cdot 8\n\\]\n\\[\na_1 \\cdot a_2 = 56\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of the first two terms is \\boxed{56}.",
