      "text": "Given that the fifth term of an arithmetic sequence is \\(11\\) and the common difference is \\(1\\), we need to determine the first term and then find the product of the first two terms.\n\nFirst, let's denote the first term of the arithmetic sequence by \\(a\\). The \\(n\\)-th term of an arithmetic sequence is given by the formula:\n\\[a_n = a + (n-1)d\\]\nwhere \\(a_n\\) is the \\(n\\)-th term, \\(a\\) is the first term, \\(d\\) is the common difference, and \\(n\\) is the term number.\n\nFor the fifth term (\\(n = 5\\)), we have:\n\\[a_5 = a + (5-1)d = a + 4d\\]\nWe know that the fifth term is \\(11\\), so:\n\\[a + 4d = 11\\]\n\nWe are also given that the common difference \\(d = 1\\). Substituting \\(d = 1\\) into the equation, we get:\n\\[a + 4 \\cdot 1 = 11\\]\n\\[a + 4 = 11\\]\n\\[a = 7\\]\n\nNow, we need to find the first two terms of the sequence. The first term is \\(a = 7\\), and the second term is:\n\\[a_2 = a + (2-1)d = 7 + 1 \\cdot 1 = 8\\]\n\nThe product of the first two terms is:\n\\[7 \\times 8 = 56\\]\n\nThus, the product of the first two terms is \\(\\boxed{56}\\).",
